WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.810
It's great to welcome to the program today,
Roy Germano, who is an author, documentary

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:05.910
filmmaker.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:12.450
And why you? Professor Roy has some great
videos on his YouTube channel where he, um,

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:18.930
is hanging out with a rabbi, a very charismatic
rabbi in the crown Heights Brooklyn neighborhood.

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:24.199
And so, so talk to us about that role. I mean,
how did, how did that idea start of going,

00:00:24.199 --> 00:00:29.800
you actually go to a one night of Hanukkah
with a family in crown Heights. How did this

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:30.970
all start?

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:35.910
It started with the tour that the rabbi gives.
So I live in crown Heights. I live about two

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:40.890
subway stops from where he lives. And I just
noticed that he was giving these tours of

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:45.730
his neighborhood and I'd always been curious
about Hasidic Jews having lived in Brooklyn

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:50.890
for 10 years and also feeling like people
like me and people like him don't usually

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:57.280
talk. So I took my stepdaughter on the tour,
we loved it. And uh, through that rabbi Katz

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:02.140
and I started hanging out. Like we would just
get coffee and talk about meaning of life

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:07.780
and stuff like that. So I'm not Jewish, I'm
not religious. Um, so it was just, it was

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:12.600
just a great experience to, to talk to somebody
that I normally wouldn't be talking to like

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.950
that. So one day we got the idea to make a
video of us hanging out and it did pretty

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:19.110
well on YouTube, so we decided to make more.

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:23.850
Yeah. And w one of the things I've talked
about for years is the, the kind of mixed

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:31.020
feeling I have when, you know, I hear that
a, a religious conservative from Iowa, uh,

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:36.460
who has been anti-gay of politically for 20
years of has finds out that they have a, a

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:42.609
child who is LGBT and then their view radically
changes because now it touches them personally.

00:01:42.609 --> 00:01:47.780
And on the one hand it's great that they are
not so far gone that a personal connection

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:53.899
can't change their mind. But at the same time,
I wish it didn't require that to be able to

00:01:53.899 --> 00:02:00.429
put ourselves in the shoes of others and imagine
what it would be like. So that I'm curious

00:02:00.429 --> 00:02:06.329
to start with, did your experience hanging
out with the Hasidic Jewish community in Brooklyn?

00:02:06.329 --> 00:02:12.840
Did it change your perspective in some sense?
On any broader social or political issue that

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:15.150
might relate to that community?

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:20.020
Yeah, I mean, what you're describing is rooted
in a social psychology research. There was

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:24.791
a great book called the nature of prejudice,
prejudice by Gordon Alport from the, and it's

00:02:24.791 --> 00:02:29.010
really about this idea that it's hard to hate
somebody that you get to know. That's sort

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:34.560
of my paraphrase of, of Alport's theory and
I think it's true. I mean a lot of us have

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:39.569
difficulty imagining what life is like for
others. So to spend time with them at the

00:02:39.569 --> 00:02:43.540
end of the day we ended up seeing that they're
not just a member of a group, but they're

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:47.790
a human being and they're an individual and
they have their own differences with that

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:52.460
group and their own similarities. And uh,
you, you can choose to dislike them or like

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:57.440
them on the basis of them as an individual,
not as a member of a group. Uh, for me personally,

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:02.329
I think by hanging out in the Hasidic neighborhood
here in crown Heights, I've just learned that

00:03:02.329 --> 00:03:07.890
the people who live there or normal people,
I guess for me, and for a lot of people here

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:13.430
in Brooklyn, you pass Hasidic people all the
time and you just assume that perhaps they

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:16.280
think and talk completely differently.

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:20.969
And then when you sit down and you, you realize
that, you know, we're all just sort of trying

00:03:20.969 --> 00:03:25.780
to figure things out on our own and they do
it their way. We do it our way. So I'd say

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:29.599
that's been the number one thing is just realizing
that they're like regular people just like

00:03:29.599 --> 00:03:30.599
you and me.

00:03:30.599 --> 00:03:35.230
A lot of the realizing that they're regular
people thing in terms of American politics

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:40.290
plays a huge role in the immigration debate
that that is now being had. You've spent time

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:47.170
in, in Mexico, uh, you talk about immigration
a lot. What is the, what are the ways in which

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:52.800
the experience with the Hasidic community
in crown Heights has sort of informed or made

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:56.999
you think about what is happening in terms
of the immigration debate? Right now, particularly

00:03:56.999 --> 00:04:00.980
from Mexico and, and points further South
in central and Latin America.

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:05.829
It's almost the reverse. It's my experience
with Mexican and central American immigrants

00:04:05.829 --> 00:04:11.629
has that has informed my approach to the hate
crime and an issue here in crown Heights.

00:04:11.629 --> 00:04:18.680
So, um, about 15, 60, however many years ago,
I was a waiter in Chicago and I worked with

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:23.480
a lot of undocumented immigrants from Mexico
at that restaurant. And to that point in my

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:28.340
life, I had never met anybody from Mexico
and I had never met an undocumented person.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:32.900
And so it was like this process of realizing,
Hey, these guys that I'm working with are

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:37.800
just normal people. They're not the kind of,
you know, drug cartel, you know, henchmen

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:45.000
that you see and, and movies. Um, they didn't
fit this stereotype of Mexico that I had seen

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:49.220
in the movies throughout my life. They were
funny. They were kind, they were intelligent,

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:52.960
they were impressive people. So it was through
that process of working at the restaurant

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:58.710
that I started to see the humanity of immigrants
and um, and just really spent a big part of

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:04.120
my career going to Mexico, learning about
the immigration issue and, and then eventually

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:08.530
making films about it to kind of bring that
information back here to the United States.

00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:13.289
So this crown Heights project is really just
an extension of a project that I started in

00:05:13.289 --> 00:05:15.970
Mexico 15 years ago.

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:19.401
When I look at the conversations that happen
about immigration and politics, whether you're

00:05:19.401 --> 00:05:24.180
looking at left or right, there are some that
always come up. What is, what is the right

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:28.910
number of people that should be allowed in?
What are we allowed to do? What should we

00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:33.800
be doing at the border in terms of protection?
How much of a focus should overstayed visas

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:39.080
be? Which source countries should we give
preference to in terms of allowing people?

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:45.600
And for me, the big thing that's missing is
the discussion of what is it that is generating

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:50.470
the desire to come to the United States in
different countries. And it seems to me that

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:55.990
actually putting resources into dealing with
that may do way more than any of the other

00:05:55.990 --> 00:06:00.379
piecemeal things that are being heavily debated.
What's your sense of that and what are the

00:06:00.379 --> 00:06:04.530
broader, broader sort of economic questions
around the issue of immigration?

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:09.490
Exactly. I think you hit the nail on the head
there that when I would go to Mexican communities,

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:14.120
back when I was doing my PhD research, I would
hear the same story over and over. It was

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:20.180
usually something like we used to grow X crop.
Then suddenly almost overnight the price of

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:26.590
that crop fell in half and then people here
could no longer make a living and so that

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:30.820
that price drop that people were talking about
was usually related to larger changes in the

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:37.410
global economy. Sort of the Neo liberalization
of the global economy, the loosening of regulations,

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:42.780
the cutting of social welfare, welfare programs,
and literally the first community I ever went

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:47.360
to in Mexico was a coffee growing community.
And people would talk about how the price

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:51.891
dropped overnight. And when I looked at the
larger changes that were happening, I saw

00:06:51.891 --> 00:06:55.389
that that had a lot to do with us foreign
economic policy.

00:06:55.389 --> 00:06:58.990
And then later I would talk to corn farmers
and they would talk about the price of corn

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:03.310
dropping. And I would see that that would
have a lot to do with NAFTA and the export

00:07:03.310 --> 00:07:08.789
of us corn to Mexico and importation into
Mexico. So I started to get the sense that

00:07:08.789 --> 00:07:14.009
if you even tweak small things in our, in
our foreign economic policy with countries

00:07:14.009 --> 00:07:18.560
like Mexico and central America, you could
see a vast reduction in the number of people

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:23.750
who are migrating without documentation. And
then the central American case is, is slightly

00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:28.440
different because there's the violence issue
there. But you can even look at how our environmental

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:33.290
policies contribute to climate change, which
has contributed to massive droughts since

00:07:33.290 --> 00:07:37.830
storms central America. That if in turn made
it harder for people to make a living, which

00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:41.900
then has resulted in more people coming to
the U S so everything's interconnected in

00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:42.900
that sense.

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:49.090
I want to try to make an analogy between immigration,
uh, in the United States to trade policy as

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:55.139
it relates to China. So we now are dealing
with the, the trade, uh, a war that is being

00:07:55.139 --> 00:07:59.580
waged against China. And we hear about, Oh,
we have a trade deficit and Donald Trump wants

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:03.810
to fix the trade deficit. And it's a very
micro focus. And if you step back, you realize

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:09.580
that for decades we've had an economy based
on we want cheap stuff manufactured overseas,

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:14.210
that that's sort of the basis. And that's
a lot of the reason why we have a trade deficit

00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:18.200
today. And if we fix the trade deficit, all
of these other things will have to change.

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:21.689
It won't necessarily be good or bad, but it
will have to change and it's not being accounted

00:08:21.689 --> 00:08:27.560
for in the same way. The analogy I would make
to immigration in the United States is that

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:34.000
a lot of those people that I see arguing for
the, for the sort of strict right-wing policies,

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:41.219
uh, that, that we've been seeing don't seem
to account for the reality of industries that

00:08:41.219 --> 00:08:46.920
are heavily reliant on both documented and
undocumented immigrant labor.

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:50.980
And even if we take them at their face that
these are the lists of things that they want

00:08:50.980 --> 00:08:55.510
to fix, there seems to be very little consideration
for the broader economic impact that that

00:08:55.510 --> 00:09:00.020
would have. And it's not, you know, you start
to get a little bit, I don't want to start

00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:03.930
saying look at the restaurant industry and
agriculture and, and sort of [inaudible] and

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:11.060
uh, uh, PR creating these stereotypes necessarily,
but there's a reality that certain industries

00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:16.430
would be very different were it not for the
status quo that exists today. How should we

00:09:16.430 --> 00:09:20.780
be thinking about that at the individual level
and how should policymakers think about that?

00:09:20.780 --> 00:09:24.870
Yeah. This is actually an area where I think
that there could be a lot of common ground

00:09:24.870 --> 00:09:30.160
between Democrats and Republicans. If you
do look at, uh, the ways in which some of

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:34.120
our communities depend upon immigrants. I
made a documentary a few years ago when I

00:09:34.120 --> 00:09:38.750
was working with vice news about the dairy
industry in upstate New York and that there's

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:43.730
so many people actually that are very politically
conservative. They're farm owners, but they've

00:09:43.730 --> 00:09:47.910
come to appreciate the contributions that
immigrants make to their community because

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:54.779
they know that they can't find people to do
entry level, uh, dairy farming work from the

00:09:54.779 --> 00:09:59.240
local community. But there are so many people
that actually have the skills that are coming

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:04.089
from agricultural communities in central America
and Mexico that are interested in doing that

00:10:04.089 --> 00:10:08.600
work. And, um, it's just amazing. It goes
back to what I said at the beginning that

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:10.540
it's hard to hate somebody that you get to
know.

00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:16.070
You see these politically conservative farmers
becoming over the years, very close with the

00:10:16.070 --> 00:10:22.490
people that work on their farms. I met farmers
who actually traveled to Mexico to go to weddings

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:26.990
and meet the extended families of the people
that have worked on their farms for, you know,

00:10:26.990 --> 00:10:31.779
10 or 20 years. Um, so in some sense I, I
think that, you know, you have that economic

00:10:31.779 --> 00:10:36.700
reality and, and we could come together on
that, but also it's a process of, of getting

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:42.579
to know each other. I think could, could contribute
to, to policy outcomes that lead to more compassionate,

00:10:42.579 --> 00:10:44.850
uh, stance on immigration.

00:10:44.850 --> 00:10:49.650
What do you think the priorities should be
in terms of what the U S can do? And that

00:10:49.650 --> 00:10:54.690
could be what we can do within our borders,
but also we have decisions that we can make

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:59.380
about how we want to work with other countries
that are sources of immigration to the United

00:10:59.380 --> 00:11:04.270
States is they're lower. The lowest hanging
fruit that you see that are the, these things

00:11:04.270 --> 00:11:05.630
should be done right away.

00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:11.070
Um, you know, it is tricky in terms of working,
for example, with certain central American

00:11:11.070 --> 00:11:15.350
countries. I mean, there's a huge culture
of corruption in a country like Honduras for

00:11:15.350 --> 00:11:20.000
example, where I did some work, but there
are a number of, of organizations down there

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:25.529
that are working to reduce gang violence.
So, uh, working to bolster those organizations

00:11:25.529 --> 00:11:32.260
and an invest in those organizations. Um,
there are, uh, various educational, uh, organizations.

00:11:32.260 --> 00:11:35.611
Like for example, there's a, an organization
that I'm familiar with called Becca, which

00:11:35.611 --> 00:11:41.240
is a, um, uh, an organization in Honduras
that provides bilingual education to young

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:45.360
people. And they've had such a great record
where none of their students have migrated,

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:50.850
uh, illegally to the United States. They found
work and central America. So I think working

00:11:50.850 --> 00:11:54.769
with those organizations would be a great,
great first step

00:11:54.769 --> 00:12:01.300
when it comes to the economic arguments about,
uh, immigrants are sending money back to their

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:06.300
home countries. And this is a drain on the
United States. I, I sort of have two questions

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:12.040
on that. One is, is it true that it is a drain
and do we not have to account for the other

00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:17.550
side of the equation, which is that particularly
undocumented immigrants are often paying into

00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:22.760
social welfare systems that because they are
undocumented, they will never take benefits

00:12:22.760 --> 00:12:26.180
out and are actually bolstering some of those
systems economically.

00:12:26.180 --> 00:12:31.350
Yeah. So I wrote a book called outsourcing
welfare. It's all about the money that immigrants

00:12:31.350 --> 00:12:36.850
sent to their home countries. And it's really
is, you know, a massive source of aid to developing

00:12:36.850 --> 00:12:41.199
countries throughout the world, not just Mexico
and central America, but throughout the world.

00:12:41.199 --> 00:12:46.010
It's worth three times more than the aid that
the world's richest countries donate to the

00:12:46.010 --> 00:12:51.690
developing world. So it's just a huge, huge
source of income for, for low and middle income

00:12:51.690 --> 00:12:55.860
countries. Um, I do get this question a lot.
Is it a drain on the United States to see

00:12:55.860 --> 00:13:01.490
this money go out? Um, you know, compared
to the GDP and the size of the United States

00:13:01.490 --> 00:13:05.829
economy, it's really a drop in the bucket.
The amount of money going out. This money

00:13:05.829 --> 00:13:10.949
does create jobs. Actually, there's so many
startups in the FinTech world and of course

00:13:10.949 --> 00:13:15.040
you have like Western union and MoneyGram
and those businesses that make a lot of money

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:17.910
off the transfer of migrant money.

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:22.800
I do think their fee should be reduced. But
that's just my perspective on that. And um,

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:28.240
and then a lot of times people are buying
American products, even in migrants homelands

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:33.240
with, with this money. So it's not like the
money completely goes away in terms of the

00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:38.560
money that immigrants pay into the system.
I actually interviewed the chief statistician

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:43.480
at the social security administration a few
years ago, and he estimates that undocumented

00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:47.740
immigrants pay about $13 billion into the
social security system and they'll never see

00:13:47.740 --> 00:13:52.270
that money back. And the way that that happens
is that a lot of undocumented immigrants get

00:13:52.270 --> 00:13:58.050
jobs using fake social security numbers and
then they're on normal payrolls and they're

00:13:58.050 --> 00:14:03.610
getting paid a paycheck. And out of that money
comes the payroll taxes, which go to the,

00:14:03.610 --> 00:14:09.600
to the federal government. So, uh, you know,
undocumented immigrants pay all kinds of taxes

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:14.930
as well. They pay, uh, you know, sales taxes
and property taxes and, and all that as well.

00:14:14.930 --> 00:14:19.020
So it's not like they're just living off of
the system as, as many people would want to

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:20.430
believe.

00:14:20.430 --> 00:14:24.519
I want to touch on one whenever it is that
the current president leaves office, whether

00:14:24.519 --> 00:14:30.339
it's in January of 2021 or January of 2025
or sometime in between or before at this point,

00:14:30.339 --> 00:14:35.949
it's anyone's guess when it's going to happen.
What are the things that most immediately

00:14:35.949 --> 00:14:40.769
should be reversed that he has done when it
comes to immigration?

00:14:40.769 --> 00:14:46.600
Well, I mean, his, his effect on the immigration
system is not just what he's done in terms

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:51.800
of hard policy, but it's kind of the chaos
that he's created in the system. For example,

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:56.420
I have, uh, friends and colleagues that work
in refugee resettlement. And for them it's

00:14:56.420 --> 00:15:00.100
just sort of an open question of what the
heck is gonna happen, right? Nobody really

00:15:00.100 --> 00:15:04.860
knows how many refugees are going to be able
to resettle. So he's managed to just really

00:15:04.860 --> 00:15:10.860
gum up the works and create all kinds of uncertainty.
So there's that. Um, I think the wall, for

00:15:10.860 --> 00:15:15.640
example, it's just ridiculous. And I've been
saying this for years. I made a video in 2010

00:15:15.640 --> 00:15:21.180
that you can find on YouTube of two Americans
climbing the wall and less than 18 seconds.

00:15:21.180 --> 00:15:25.750
And I've always called the wall assemble in
the desert. It's just this very expensive

00:15:25.750 --> 00:15:29.760
symbol that some politicians want to build
so that they say that they're doing something

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:35.360
about the issue. You could take all of those
billions of dollars and invest that in communities

00:15:35.360 --> 00:15:42.190
in a place like Honduras and see huge, huge,
uh, uh, return on that investment and terms

00:15:42.190 --> 00:15:47.620
of people's lives being improved. And then
fewer people coming to the United States.

00:15:47.620 --> 00:15:52.000
We've been speaking with NYU, professor Roy
Germano, who's an [inaudible]

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:56.310
author, documentary filmmaker. Uh, you can
check out some of his work by if you are watching

00:15:56.310 --> 00:16:00.069
on YouTube, checking out the links down in
the video description. Roy is such a pleasure

00:16:00.069 --> 00:16:01.069
having you on. I really do

00:16:01.069 --> 00:16:02.850
appreciate your time. Thanks David. Thanks
so much.

